%n [nippu]š? | mīnâ | ibrī
#note: See @bib{RN117@330}: “iii 4'. The restoration is very provisional, suggested by SB X 86: @i{ana mê mūti kī taktaldu teppuš mīna}. The line of poetry continued on to the next line of tablet.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "X" 86

%n [kī] nippala | kamrāti | [nišī]
#note: See @bib{RN117@330}: “iii 5'–6'. The line of poetry restored over these two lines is used by Enkidu in a similar predicament in SB VI 131: @i{kī nippala kamrāti ni}[@i{šī}]. On the reversal of nouns and adjectives see Chapter 10, the section on Some features of language and style.”
// L I.4 SB "VI" 131

%n [...] | šittā | šahnā
#note: See @bib{RN117@330}: “iii 7'. Without good context the word @i{ša-aḫ-na} is open to several interpretations (for @i{aḫ} similarly written in a literary tablet from Emar see, e.g., Msk 74143m, obv. 4' = Tamarisk and Date-Palm). I read @i{šaʾnā}, a stative verbal form in Assyrian dialect. Arnaud and Bottéro took it from the noun @i{šēnu}, ‘sandal’. It can be derived just as easily from @i{šaḫānu}, ‘to be warm’.”
// cf. L I.4 MB "Ug₂" a+3

%n [...] ... | lū mušhalṣītum-ma | šina | [(...)]
#note: See @bib{RN117@330}: “iii 8'. The rare word @i{mušḫalṣītu} is literally ‘that which causes one to slip’ (cf. its Sumerian counterpart @sux{ki ba.an.ze₂.er}), prompting the translations ‘slippery ground’ (@i{CAD} M/2, s.v.) and ‘ein schlüpfrige Stelle?’ (@i{AHw}, p. 775). It occurs in a suggestive context in a section of a lexical list that treats the defences of a city: @sux{ki ba.an.ze₂.er} = @akk{muš-hal-ṣ[i-tum]}, @sux{SIG₄{+u₂-rum}.BAD} = @akk{in-[du]} (‘buttress, abutment’), @sux{nam.us₂} = @akk{ne₂-met-[tum]} (‘rampart’), @i{MSL} XVII, p. 196, @i{Antagal} C 49–51. For this reason I assume that it is not just any slippery place but the technical term for a glacis, the smooth slope at the base of a defensive rampart which is designed at once to reinforce the wall and to hamper the approach of enemy forces, siege engines, towers and ladders. The point of the line would appear to be that while a man cannot expect to climb such a slope alone, two men helping each other have a good chance of success.”
// L I.4 SB "V" 77
